    id: "2bc59e163eb694667401",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Children brave snipers and mortars to go to school near Yemen front line",
    summary: "In rare access to Taiz, the BBC World Service speaks to children, teachers and families affected by the war.",
    preview: "Every morning at 6am, 10-year-old Rafeef and her younger brother get ready for school, putting on their cream and brown uniforms. She always looks forward to seeing her friends and learning about everything from maths to Arabic and Islamic studies. She can't wait to start studying English next year. But for Rafeef, the journey to school involves far greater risks than crossing roads or missing the bus.",
    body: "Every morning at 6am, 10-year-old Rafeef and her younger brother get ready for school, putting on their cream and brown uniforms.\n\nShe always looks forward to seeing her friends and learning about everything from maths to Arabic and Islamic studies. She can't wait to start studying English next year.\n\nBut for Rafeef, the journey to school involves far greater risks than crossing roads or missing the bus.\n\nHer school in Taiz, south-western Yemen, stands on a mountain at the edge of a valley known as no-man's land - a heavily contested front line in the civil war between forces supporting Yemen's internationally recognised government, which is backed by Saudi Arabia, and the Iran-backed Houthis.\n\nOn one side of the school is government-held territory, on the other are Houthi sniper positions.\n\nRafeef's home is further down the mountain.\n\nEvery time she goes to school, she has to walk uphill with her back towards the snipers. \"I'm afraid,\" she says.\n\nAccording to Unicef, roughly 3.2 million children in Yemen were out of school even before the war escalated last month.\n\nIt says thousands of children have been killed or maimed since the war began in 2014, while thousands more have been recruited by armed groups on both sides.\n\nYet despite the risks, Rafeef says she does not want to stop going because she loves seeing her friends and it is her only chance to be with them. She begins naming the children in her classroom.\n\nGoing to school is the only time Rafeef's parents let her go outside. They say they are keen for her to get an education and have the chance of a better life. Rafeef hopes to become a doctor.\n\n'A mortar came out of nowhere'\n\nRafeef's family moved to Taiz from the coastal city of Hudaydah nine years ago, seeking safety when Houthis took control there.\n\nBut Taiz has also endured fighting.\n\nRafeef says that two years ago there was a mortar attack outside her new home.\n\n\"Everyone was out except for me and my brother. Then a mortar came out of nowhereâ¦ shrapnel blew through the door and into the house.\" Her arm was broken and there is still a scar where she was hit.\n\nAnd in recent days the conflict has intensified. On Sunday the Houthis cut off a vital supply route linking Taiz to Aden, which meant they were able to effectively encircle Taiz. Control of that route has kept shifting.\n\nResidents have told the BBC they are afraid the Houthis could seize the city at any moment.\n\nGovernment forces have made gains in other areas this week, they say, retaking control of strategic points on the Bab al-Mandab Strait - a key shipping chokepoint - and launching an offensive to try to regain control of the capital, Sanaa.\n\n- Who are the Houthis and what do they want?\n\n- Published24 September\n\n- Yemeni military says it has 'secured' Red Sea waterway\n\n- Published7 hours ago\n\nHomes in Taiz are mostly scattered across the slopes, but many have been abandoned by families fleeing the fighting.\n\nTwice, we try to visit the neighbourhood where Rafeef and her family live, but a community leader advises us it is too dangerous.\n\nOn the first occasion, residents of the neighbourhood tell us that three people have been shot by snipers. On the second, we are told of another shooting.\n\nAt Rafeef's school, one of the teachers, Abdel Raqeeb al-Obaidi, 45, takes us on to the rooftop to show us what he says are Houthi military camps, barracks and sniper positions in the mountains.\n\nStanding on the rooftop, a Yemeni security adviser accompanying us suddenly tells us to go back inside. We are visible from these positions and could be shot, he says.\n\nAbdel Raqeeb laughs, acknowledging the danger. He says the school has been fired on before.\n\n\"The situation may explode at any moment while we are sitting in class or sleeping or we are in school or we are out on the street. We do not know how our future is and how tomorrow is.\"\n\nFor Abdel Raqeeb, the risk has become routine. His familiarity with it is unsettling.\n\nTaiz has long been a major battleground in the country's devastating civil war. After ousting the government from the capital Sanaa in 2015, the Houthis attempted to also seize control of Taiz but were driven out of most of the city by local militiamen.\n\nSeizing Taiz would give the Houthis full control over the country's north. Now, in their latest offensive, Houthis are making extensive use of drones alongside other weapons.\n\nSometimes civilians are collateral damage in the fighting.\n\nLater that evening, we get a call from a hospital about a five minute drive away from the school. Two children have been badly injured by a mortar strike.\n\nWhen we arrive at Al-Rawdah Hospital, we see a nurse standing outside the intensive care unit, holding a tiny pair of beige, rubber sandals that are splattered with blood.\n\nThey belonged to Ahmed, a three-year-old boy who we learn was killed when a mortar struck a mechanic's shop in the city.\n\nShrapnel in the heart\n\nWhen I ask about the shoes, the nurse, Ikram Shamsan, begins to cry.\n\n\"What did this child or his family ever do wrong?\" she asks.\n\nAhmed was killed when a piece of shrapnel pierced his heart, entering through his back. The boy had been with his father, who was getting his motorcycle tyre pumped up at the mechanic's shop. The father was unharmed.\n\nA doctor tells us the father had intended to leave his son at home, but the boy cried because he wanted to come too.\n\nAs he takes his son's body from the hospital, the father asks how he will face the boy's mother.\n\nCompleting a simple errand turned into the worst day of their lives.\n\nAnother boy, 11-year-old Amjad, was also at the mechanic's shop. He and his brother worked there with their uncle, sending money to their family in their home village.\n\nShrapnel has struck Amjad in his lung and abdomen. When we reach him, he is in intensive care, struggling to breathe. No relatives are beside his bed.\n\nHis doctor describes his condition as critical and fears he could die within hours. He hopes his mother will arrive in time to say goodbye.\n\nAmjad was admitted at about 20:00 that evening. His mother reaches him at around 06:00 the following morning, delayed by the eight-hour journey from the village.\n\nShe sits beside his bed and prays, trying to remain composed. Once outside the room, she falls to her knees and breaks down.\n\nDoctors say his heart rate improves during her visit. They judge him stable enough for another operation.\n\nHe has since undergone that surgery, but remains critically ill.\n\n\"We're always terrified. It's devastating to see children killed,\" says nurse Shamsan. \"We want peace and we want to be safe.\"\n\nAdditional\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published27 September",
